package com.hpcai270

// Days 237–253: cloud HPC/AI, hybrid architecture, infrastructure-as-code and economics.
val hpcDaysPart20 = listOf(
Day(237,"Cloud HPC architecture","Understand the cloud as infrastructure","Cloud HPC still has compute, network, storage, identity and orchestration. The provider operates physical infrastructure; you still own workload architecture and configuration.","MODEL\n\nCloud region → network/VPC → GPU instances → high-performance filesystem → object storage → IAM → monitoring.","TRADE-OFF\n\nCloud provides elasticity and managed services but introduces network/storage service limits, pricing, quotas and egress considerations.","LAB\n\nDesign a cloud training environment with GPU compute, object storage and a parallel filesystem. Mark control and data paths.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat changes when moving HPC to cloud? Hardware operations decrease, while architecture, automation, IAM, cost and service limits become more important.","GOAL\n\nThink cloud-native without forgetting HPC physics."),
Day(238,"GCP storage for AI","Object, file, block and cache choices","Cloud storage types map to workload semantics. Object storage provides durable scalable data; file services provide shared filesystem semantics; block storage supports attached volumes.","DECISION\n\nObject → durable datasets/artifacts. File/parallel FS → shared active workloads. Block/local NVMe → low-latency node-local or application-specific data.","AI\n\nUse lifecycle tiers and cache hot data rather than forcing every byte into the most expensive performance tier.","LAB\n\nMap five AI data types to cloud storage classes and explain access pattern, durability and cost considerations.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy might cloud object storage be the source of truth while a parallel filesystem is the training working set? Different semantics and performance goals.","GOAL\n\nBecome fluent in cloud storage architecture."),
Day(239,"Cloud parallel filesystems","Elastic HPC data planes","Cloud HPC can expose managed or marketplace parallel filesystems. Learn the architecture and the operational implications of elastic scale.","FLOW\n\nGPU instances → high-speed cloud network → parallel filesystem → object source/archive.","PERFORMANCE\n\nService quotas, client count, network placement and filesystem throughput limits matter. ‘Managed’ does not mean infinite.","LAB\n\nDesign a cloud training workload and identify the storage/network metrics you would monitor.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat should you verify before moving an HPC workload to a cloud filesystem? Throughput, metadata, latency, concurrent clients, mount model, availability and cost.","GOAL\n\nTreat cloud managed storage as a service with measurable limits."),
Day(240,"Hybrid HPC/AI","On-prem plus cloud","Hybrid architectures can keep high-performance datasets on-prem while using cloud GPUs for burst capacity or disaster recovery.","PATTERN\n\nOn-prem source → high-speed/cloud interconnect → cloud compute → cache/parallel FS → results → controlled return/archive.","CHALLENGE\n\nData transfer can dominate economics and time. Design around data gravity and repeated movement.","LAB\n\nEvaluate bursting 500 TB to cloud. Estimate transfer time from a hypothetical 100 Gbps link and identify recurring data movement risks.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhen is cloud bursting a bad idea? When datasets are huge, transfer bandwidth is limited, workload is steady and on-prem economics are better.","GOAL\n\nUse hybrid cloud intentionally, not because it sounds modern."),
Day(241,"Cloud networking for AI","VPC, subnets, routes and high-speed fabrics","Cloud AI networks still need topology, routing, security and performance. GPU clusters often have specialized high-bandwidth networking options.","LAYERS\n\nVPC/VNet → subnet → route → NIC → high-speed fabric → storage/service endpoint.","SECURITY\n\nLeast-privilege firewall rules, private endpoints, identity-aware access and separate management/data paths.","LAB\n\nDraw a cloud AI VPC with management, compute and storage paths. Identify internet-facing versus private components.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can a cloud storage service be fast but still produce poor training performance? Placement, quotas, application concurrency and network path matter.","GOAL\n\nTransfer physical networking discipline into cloud design."),
Day(242,"Cloud IAM","Identity becomes the new control plane","In cloud, identity and policy control many infrastructure actions. Storage engineers must understand service accounts, roles, policies and resource hierarchy.","MODEL\n\nHuman/service identity → role/policy → resource → audit event.","AI\n\nTraining jobs need controlled access to datasets, checkpoints, registries and secrets. Avoid broad project-wide permissions.","LAB\n\nDesign least-privilege permissions for a training job that can read datasets and write checkpoints but cannot modify production infrastructure.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is IAM a storage skill in cloud? Because data access is enforced through identity and policy as well as filesystem/object permissions.","GOAL\n\nBe comfortable reviewing cloud access policies."),
Day(243,"Terraform fundamentals","Infrastructure as code","Terraform describes infrastructure declaratively. It helps make cloud environments repeatable, reviewable and recoverable.","MODEL\n\nConfiguration → plan → review → apply → state → drift detection.","AI\n\nEncode networks, storage, GPU instances, IAM and monitoring rather than creating them manually.","OPERATIONS\n\nRemote state, locking, modules, variables, outputs and secrets management are production concerns.","LAB\n\nCreate a simple Terraform module for a network/storage resource. Run plan, review the diff and apply in a test project.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is Terraform valuable for HPC? A 100-node environment should be reproducible rather than rebuilt manually after an outage.","GOAL\n\nMake infrastructure change reviewable as code."),
Day(244,"Terraform for storage","Declarative storage architecture","Storage resources, IAM, network paths and lifecycle policies can be encoded as infrastructure. The goal is consistent desired state, not replacing every operational task with Terraform.","DESIGN\n\nModule → variables → environment-specific configuration → plan → approval → apply.","RISK\n\nSome storage operations are asynchronous/destructive or not safely modeled as simple resource replacement. Understand provider behavior before automation.","LAB\n\nCreate a lifecycle module for object storage plus IAM and monitoring. Add validation for naming and retention policy.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow do you prevent Terraform from accidentally destroying production storage? Review plans, lifecycle controls, state protection, policy and approval gates.","GOAL\n\nAutomate safely, not blindly."),
Day(245,"Cloud cost engineering","Performance has a price","AI infrastructure cost includes GPU time, storage capacity, I/O operations, network transfer, idle resources and managed service premiums.","MODEL\n\nCost = compute + storage + network + managed services + operations.\n\nThe cheapest GB is not always the cheapest training run if it starves expensive GPUs.","AI\n\nA faster storage tier can be economically better if it reduces GPU idle time enough to shorten training.","LAB\n\nCompare two hypothetical architectures: expensive storage with 90% GPU utilization versus cheaper storage with 55%. Identify the missing variables needed for a real decision.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow would you calculate storage ROI? Include application throughput, GPU utilization, training time, storage cost and operational cost.","GOAL\n\nSpeak about performance in economic terms."),
Day(246,"Cloud quotas and service limits","The hidden architecture constraint","Cloud services impose quotas: GPU availability, network capacity, filesystem throughput, API requests and storage limits. Architecture must respect them.","PROCESS\n\nRequirement → service limits → quota request → architecture adjustment → validation.","FAILURE\n\nA design can be technically valid but impossible to deploy because regional GPU capacity or a service quota is unavailable.","LAB\n\nCreate a pre-deployment checklist of cloud quotas for a 256-GPU cluster.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy should capacity planning include quotas? Because cloud resources are not infinite and allocation can be region/project dependent.","GOAL\n\nTreat cloud limits as architecture inputs."),
Day(247,"Cloud object lifecycle","Hot to cold data economics","Object lifecycle policies can move data between storage classes based on age or access pattern.","MODEL\n\nActive → standard/hot → infrequent → archive/cold → deletion according to policy.","AI\n\nCompleted checkpoints and raw datasets may have very different retention and access needs.","RISK\n\nMoving data to cold tiers can increase retrieval latency and cost. Policies must match real access patterns.","LAB\n\nDesign lifecycle rules for training data, checkpoints, logs and final models.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is lifecycle policy a storage architecture decision rather than a finance-only decision? Because it changes data availability and application behavior.","GOAL\n\nCombine performance and cost lifecycle thinking."),
Day(248,"Cloud monitoring","Metrics, logs and billing telemetry","Cloud monitoring must correlate infrastructure performance with cost and service health.","DASHBOARD\n\nGPU utilization, storage throughput, latency, filesystem health, network traffic, API errors, job duration and cost.","AI\n\nLow GPU utilization with normal storage may indicate application or scheduler problems. High cost with low utilization indicates idle capacity.","LAB\n\nDesign a daily AI infrastructure report containing performance and cost signals.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhich metric would you alert on first: storage latency, GPU utilization or cost? It depends on the service objective; explain the relationship.","GOAL\n\nOperate cloud AI as a measurable service."),
Day(249,"Cloud security architecture","Defense in depth","Secure cloud AI platforms using identity, network isolation, encryption, secrets, image security and audit.","LAYERS\n\nIAM → private networking → encryption → key management → image/cluster security → data governance → monitoring.","STORAGE\n\nSeparate public exposure from private datasets. Use signed/trusted images and restricted service identities.","LAB\n\nDesign a threat model for a training bucket and GPU cluster. List five threats and controls.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is the risk of granting a training service account project-owner permissions? Blast radius and credential compromise.","GOAL\n\nTreat cloud security as architecture, not a final checklist."),
Day(250,"Cloud disaster recovery","Regional and zonal failure","Cloud does not eliminate failure. Design backups, replicas, infrastructure-as-code and recovery procedures for the required RPO/RTO.","DR MODEL\n\nPrimary region → backup/replica → infrastructure definition → data recovery → validation.","AI\n\nNot every training dataset needs multi-region replication. Unique model artifacts and critical metadata may deserve stronger protection.","LAB\n\nDesign DR for a 100 TB AI platform with RPO 24 hours and RTO 8 hours.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is ‘the cloud is highly available’ not a DR plan? Availability and recoverability address different failure scopes.","GOAL\n\nDesign recovery as a tested capability."),
Day(251,"Cloud migration methodology","Move workloads without losing performance","Migration should be measured: discover → classify → baseline → pilot → migrate → validate → optimize → retire.","DATA\n\nDetermine dataset size, change rate, access pattern, dependencies and acceptable transfer window.","AI\n\nBenchmark the application before and after migration. A successful copy is not a successful migration if training time doubles.","LAB\n\nCreate a migration plan for a 200 TB dataset and 64-GPU workload. Include rollback and validation.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is your go/no-go criterion? Define measurable performance, reliability, security and cost targets.","GOAL\n\nMigrate based on evidence, not optimism."),
Day(252,"Hybrid storage strategy","Keep the right data in the right place","Hybrid environments can use on-prem high-performance storage, cloud object storage, backup repositories and local GPU caches together.","REFERENCE\n\nSource of truth → active working set → local cache → durable checkpoint → archive.","OPERATIONS\n\nData synchronization, identity, lifecycle and monitoring must work across environments.","LAB\n\nDesign a hybrid data architecture and mark where copies are authoritative versus temporary.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow do you avoid creating five inconsistent copies of a dataset? Define ownership, versioning and lifecycle semantics.","GOAL\n\nMake hybrid storage understandable and governable."),
Day(253,"Cloud/HPC architecture checkpoint","Defend an on-prem versus cloud decision","You should be able to evaluate workload placement across on-prem, cloud and hybrid.","DECISION MATRIX\n\nUtilization, data gravity, GPU availability, storage performance, network transfer, security, cost, operations, elasticity and DR.","LAB\n\nEvaluate three workloads: steady 24×7 training, bursty research and disaster-recovery compute. Recommend placement and justify.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhen should a company keep HPC on-prem? High sustained utilization, large local datasets, predictable capacity, specialized hardware and economics can all matter.","STANDARD\n\nYou can now discuss cloud without assuming cloud is automatically cheaper or better.")
)
